==Spanish==

===Etymology===
{{inh+|es|osp|-}}, from {{inh|es|LL.|cocō|cocere}}, from {{inh|es|la|coquo|coquere}}. Some conjugated forms of the verb were reformed through analogy with the infinitive; in Old Spanish, the forms {{m|es|cueza}} and {{m|es|cuezo}} were ''cuega'' and ''cuego'', respectively, {{m|es|coció}} was ''coxo'', and the past participle was {{m|es|cocho}} instead of {{m|es|cocido}}.<ref>{{R:es:DCECH}}</ref> Cognate with {{cog|en|cook}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{es-pr|+<audio:LL-Q1321 (spa)-AdrianAbdulBaha-cocer.wav<a:Colombia>><hmp:coser<q:Latin America>>}}

===Verb===
{{es-verb}}

# to [[boil]]
# to [[cook]] {{gloss|only used in situations where the food being prepared is submitted to ''fire''; such as through the processes of boiling, simmering or steaming anything; or baking bread slowly in an oven}}
#: {{ux|es|cocer a fuego lento|inline=1|[[simmer]]}}
# {{lb|es|reflexive|figurative}} to [[brew]]
#: {{ux|es|Algo se está '''cociendo'''|Something is '''brewing'''}}
# {{lb|es|pronominal}} to [[chafe]] {{gloss|get sore}}
#: {{syn|es|[[escocer]]se|[[escaldar]]se}}

